    "narrative": "The offensive engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is anchored by **James Wood** (76.39), who projects as our premier start. Batting leadoff against Mitch Keller\u2019s 4.60 xERA, **James Wood** benefits from an elite 0.418 xwOBA and the highest order-slot boost (+10%) in our lineup. Joining him in the elite tier are **Brice Turang** (72.10) and **Isaac Paredes** (71.62). Both benefit significantly from favorable matchups against mid-rotation arms; **Brice Turang** earns a +16% boost from Jose Cabrera\u2019s weak peripherals, while **Isaac Paredes** capitalizes on the +18% SP skill edge against Nick Martinez. Meanwhile, **Mickey Moniak** (69.69) remains a high-floor play, buoyed by a substantial +14% park factor, which continues to outweigh his lack of an official lineup slot.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Josh Naylor** (45.99), who narrowly missed the cut. While his underlying talent is undeniable, the matchup against the formidable Dylan Cease (3.01 xERA) suppressed his ceiling, resulting in a -19% penalty that pushed him below the threshold for our high-leverage utility spots. We are also monitoring **Travis Bazzana** (48.67), whose potential was compromised by a 38% rain risk and a difficult left-on-left platoon disadvantage. Managers should be particularly wary of the \"Lineup Pending\" status across the board; while **Dalton Rushing**, **Alex Freeland**, and **Jose Caballero** are currently projected for mid-tier scoring, their value is heavily sensitive to late-breaking lineup changes that could further compress their projected order-slot boosts. Stay alert for official card releases, as any downward shift in order for **Max Muncy** or **Austin Riley** could necessitate a pivot to our bench depth.",
